WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.760
Here's a question I ask myself with

00:01.840 --> 00:03.600
every player I consider representing.

00:04.160 --> 00:05.760
If I had to choose between a player with

00:05.880 --> 00:07.920
incredible natural talent but low work

00:08.000 --> 00:10.240
ethic, and a player with medium talent

00:10.320 --> 00:12.400
but a relentless work ethic, which one

00:12.480 --> 00:13.040
do I take?

00:13.680 --> 00:14.640
I take the second one.

00:15.120 --> 00:16.240
Every single time.

00:16.920 --> 00:19.120
I'm Bert Mahecha, founder of MFH Global

00:19.200 --> 00:21.360
Football, and I've coached, refereed,

00:21.680 --> 00:23.440
owned a club, and represented players,

00:23.840 --> 00:25.680
so I've watched this play out over and

00:25.760 --> 00:26.080
over.

00:26.600 --> 00:28.080
Talent gets a player noticed.

00:28.440 --> 00:29.920
It does not get a player through a

00:30.040 --> 00:32.240
season of early mornings, tough coaches,

00:32.560 --> 00:34.640
missed calls, and setbacks that never

00:34.720 --> 00:35.280
seem to end.

00:35.840 --> 00:37.120
Only discipline does that.

00:37.680 --> 00:38.880
And here's the part that surprises

00:38.960 --> 00:41.120
people: talent without discipline

00:41.200 --> 00:42.800
doesn't just stall, it actually

00:42.920 --> 00:44.000
collapses under pressure.

00:44.640 --> 00:45.920
Discipline, on the other hand,

00:46.130 --> 00:46.960
compounds.

00:47.040 --> 00:48.960
It gets stronger every single week that

00:49.000 --> 00:50.960
a player shows up and does the work

00:51.040 --> 00:52.400
nobody's watching.

00:52.450 --> 00:53.760
I've seen players from comfortable

00:53.800 --> 00:56.160
situations who had every advantage and

00:56.240 --> 00:58.400
quit the moment things got hard, because

00:58.600 --> 01:00.000
comfort had never taught them how to

01:00.120 --> 01:01.440
push through difficulty.

01:01.490 --> 01:02.800
And I've seen players with almost

01:02.880 --> 01:05.120
nothing, who trained three times a day

01:05.200 --> 01:06.960
and treated every single opportunity

01:07.040 --> 01:08.880
like it might be their last, because for

01:08.960 --> 01:11.280
them, it genuinely was.

01:11.440 --> 01:13.120
That hunger isn't something you can fake

01:13.160 --> 01:15.320
in a highlight reel, and honestly, it's

01:15.360 --> 01:16.880
not something a highlight reel can even

01:17.000 --> 01:17.760
show you.

01:17.880 --> 01:19.680
So when I evaluate a player, I'm not

01:19.760 --> 01:21.200
just watching the footwork.

01:21.280 --> 01:23.120
I'm asking how they respond when a coach

01:23.200 --> 01:24.080
corrects them.

01:24.160 --> 01:26.160
I'm asking whether they show up on time,

01:26.320 --> 01:28.000
whether they finish what they start,

01:28.160 --> 01:29.600
whether they're coachable or whether

01:29.680 --> 01:31.760
they think they already know everything.

01:31.920 --> 01:33.840
Those answers matter as much as anything

01:34.000 --> 01:35.280
I see on the ball.

01:35.390 --> 01:36.720
If you're a player watching this and

01:36.760 --> 01:38.800
wondering what actually gets you signed,

01:38.880 --> 01:40.240
here's the honest answer.

01:40.320 --> 01:42.160
It's not just your highlight reel.

01:42.240 --> 01:44.560
It's who you are when nobody's filming.

01:44.640 --> 01:46.480
Build that, and the talent will get its

01:46.520 --> 01:48.000
chance to show up too.

01:48.080 --> 01:50.080
If you want an honest evaluation, one

01:50.160 --> 01:51.840
that looks at the whole player, not just

01:51.920 --> 01:53.280
the highlights, book a free

01:53.520 --> 01:54.400
consultation.

01:54.480 --> 01:56.240
We'll tell you the truth, even when it's

01:56.320 --> 01:57.440
not what you want to hear.
